# 20. Correct definition of machine-learned confidence

BEAR should never output:

```text
Death score = 84
Confidence = 84%
```

unless a calibration procedure genuinely shows that predictions in that region succeed roughly 84% of the time.

Instead define executable labels.

For a short entered at actual fill `F_t` and closed at actual executable fill `F_t+h`:

```text
raw_short_return_h =
    -(F_exit / F_entry - 1)

net_short_return_h =
    raw_short_return
    + realized_funding
    - fees
    - slippage
    - borrow/carry costs
```

Also calculate relative alpha:

```text
alpha_h =
    net_short_return_h
    - hedge_or_benchmark_return_h
```

Useful labels:

```text
Y_profit_7d   = 1[net_short_return_7d > 0]
Y_profit_30d  = 1[net_short_return_30d > 0]

Y_alpha_30d   = 1[alpha_30d > 0]
Y_alpha5_30d  = 1[alpha_30d > 5%]

Y_stop        = 1[MAE breaches risk limit before exit]

Y_tail        = 1[net_short_return_30d > 10%]
```

Continuous targets:

```text
net_short_return_7d
net_short_return_30d
alpha_30d
MFE
MAE
time_to_target
```

