# 21. Start ML simple, not with a giant model

Given BEAR's current effective sample size, sophisticated boosting will overfit easily.

Initial models should be:

### Binary outcome

Penalized logistic regression:

```text
P(net short return > 0)
P(alpha > threshold)
P(stop-out)
```

Advantages:

* interpretable;
* regularized;
* much lower variance;
* straightforward probability calibration.

### Continuous outcome

Use:

```text
Ridge
Huber regression
quantile regression
```

to estimate:

```text
E[return]
q10
q50
q90
```

Only introduce LightGBM/XGBoost after there are enough genuinely OOS time blocks to show consistent nonlinear benefit.

Complexity should have to beat the linear baseline prospectively.

