# 22. Probability calibration

A classifier's raw `predict_proba()` is not automatically trustworthy.

Generate **out-of-fold predictions** only.

Then fit a separate probability calibrator using predictions the underlying model did not train on.

Preferred initial calibration:

```text
Platt / sigmoid calibration
```

For large calibration datasets:

```text
isotonic regression
```

Scikit-learn specifically notes that isotonic can overfit when calibration datasets are small and tends to become more appropriate with larger samples, while reliability diagrams and proper scoring rules should be used to assess probability quality. ([GitHub][5])

Report:

```text
Brier score
log loss
calibration intercept
calibration slope
reliability curve
ECE if desired
```

### Example BEAR output

Not:

```text
PHA
Death Score: 86
Confidence: 91
```

Instead:

```text
PHA / 30-day short

Predicted P(net profit > 0):      0.67
Predicted P(alpha > 5%):         0.54
Expected net return:             +3.4%

Predicted distribution:
q10: -8.2%
q50: +2.9%
q90: +15.5%

Data completeness:               92%
Calibration cohort size:         184 OOS observations
Independent rebalance blocks:    41
OOD score:                       normal
Model version:                   bear-short-30d-v4
```

That is quantitative confidence.

